<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Awash River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Awash River is a river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> that flows from the central highlands towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> lowlands. In the fourteenth century Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon</a> launched expansion southward across the Awash, incorporating regions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawaro">Dawaro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bale">Bale</a> and several Muslim sultanates. The river marked an important frontier in the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/awash-river.jpg" alt="Awash River" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bjørg Sandkjær (Bjorg Sandkjaer) · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Awash_river.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Awash is about 1,200 kilometres long and runs entirely within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. It rises at an elevation of about 3,000 metres in the central highlands, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, then enters the floor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">Great Rift Valley</a>, loops around Mount Zuqualla, passes the city of Adama and the Awash National Park, and is joined by its chief tributary, the Germama or Kasam. It ends in a chain of linked lakes that begins with Lake Gargori and finishes at Lake Abbe, on the border with Djibouti, at about 250 metres. It is the principal stream of a basin with no outlet to the sea, which covers about a tenth of Ethiopia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Awash Valley, and above all the Middle Awash, has a very high density of fossil hominins, dating from roughly 5.6 to 2.5 million years ago. They include many Australopithecines, among them Lucy, together with Ardipithecus and Homo erectus. The lower valley was inscribed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s World Heritage List in 1980 for its palaeontological and anthropological importance.</p><p>In the 16th century the river was called the great Dir river and lay in the country of the Muslims. The valley from about 9° N downstream is the traditional home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> people and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issa-clan">Issa Somali</a> clan, and it has been counted as part of the historic regions of Fatagar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a> and Shewa. The first European to follow the river to its end, in the oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aussa">Aussa</a> and Lake Abbe, was Wilfred Thesiger in 1933 and 1934.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Koka Dam, completed across the river in 1960 about 75 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, provides hydroelectric power and created Lake Gelila, also called the Koka Reservoir, which covers about 180 square kilometres. The river and its tributaries supply water to Addis Ababa and other towns and to irrigated farmland, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>-cane plantations. The lower valley is one of the last refuges of the African wild ass.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Awash River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Consolidation came under Amda Seyon I, who acceded in 1314, organised the army into standing chewa regiments, and expanded southward across the <b>Awash River</b> and eastward, subduing Ifat, Dawaro, Bale, and other Muslim principalities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The sultanate controlled the irrigated lands of the lower <b>Awash</b> river, an island of farming in the deserts of the Afar Triangle.</blockquote><a href="/state/aussa-sultanate">Aussa Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Fatagar is known from Arabic and Ethiopian sources as a Muslim region lying between Shewa and the <b>Awash</b> river, east of modern Addis Ababa.</blockquote><a href="/state/fatagar-sultanate">Fatagar Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1577 Imam Muhammad Jasa moved the seat of government to the oasis of Aussa on the lower <b>Awash</b> river, in the Afar lowlands, creating a new imamate there.</blockquote><a href="/state/imamate-of-aussa">Imamate of Aussa</a></li><li><blockquote>Dawaro lay south of Ifat, between the <b>Awash</b> and the upper Wabi Shebelle river, and appears in sources from the 13th century as one of the Muslim states of the Ethiopian interior.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-dawaro">Sultanate of Dawaro</a></li></ul></section>
